m Dana Okafor, Security Operations manager, 9 March (manager-account): On 28 Feb we stopped a credential-stuffing run against web login. My read is that the Android drop is the same crowd moving onto the mobile API: bots fail logins, the success rate goes down. Two asks: (1) exclude edge-blocked attempts from the login KPI, because those are not real customers and they are distorting the number executives see; (2) raise Corvane bot-protection sensitivity on all login endpoints from 'standard' to 'high' until this blows over. Happy to brief the executive committee.\n- Digital analytics KPI definition: mobile login success rate (v3, January) (document): Login success rate = login-success events ÷ login-attempt events, both emitted by the in-app telemetry SDK. An attempt event is emitted when a user submits a password or triggers biometric/PIN quick-unlock in an installed copy of the app. Each event carries an app-install ID, app version, OS version and an outcome category: success, credentials rejected, request rejected by server, or network/timeout. Requests sent to the API by scripts or tools other than the installed app do not emit telemetry events and are not counted. Dashboard tiles show the platform-level rate only.\n- Contact-centre weekly operations summary, week of 2 March (system-record): Inbound calls 2–8 March: 41,200 against a four-week average of 34,900 (+18%). Average wait 6m40s (target 3m). Scheduled events this week: annual account-fee notices mailed 2 March to 610,000 customers; savings-rate change emailed 4 March. Reason coding: 57% of calls logged as 'General enquiry', the default code; a January coding audit found reason codes unreliable for trend analysis. Team leads report 'a mix of fee questions and people who can't get into the app'.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe Android login drop is almost certainly not caused by release 6.14. At 10:38 UTC on 3 March, Corvane auto-updated its edge rules. A new rule, AUTO-LIB-17, blocks any password-login request that uses the nettle-http library with no edge session cookie. That describes every Android app version, because the app uses nettle-http for all API calls. The rule returns a 403. It has blocked about 9,700 requests a day, from roughly 41,300 app installs, 92% of which had logged in successfully before. The failures began within a minute of the rule going live, on every app version and every OS version, whether or not the March security patch was installed. iOS, web and quick-unlock never match the rule. So the rollback of 6.14, the OS patch, the screen redesign, the auth token change and credential stuffing don't explain the drop. I'm quite confident (about 90%). Next: ask Corvane to switch AUTO-LIB-17 to log-only on the mobile password path, keeping rate limits, lockouts and other rules on. Then confirm in telemetry that password success recovers. Don't roll back 6.14. Don't raise bot sensitivity. Don't exclude blocked attempts from the KPI, because those are real customers.\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XhCnMiqte3QuNRbxuqNMoX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
